Revision history for Authen-WebAuthn
 
Unreleased
  [Behaviour changes]
  - CryptX 0.089 or later is required, for Ed448
  - Cross-origin requests (crossOrigin or topOrigin in the client data)
    are refused unless the new allow_cross_origin attribute allows them
  - At registration, a credential public key is refused if it does not
    parse, if its key type does not match its algorithm, or if it is an
    RSA key under 2048 bits
  - Token Binding is no longer checked, as no browser supports it and
    WebAuthn Level 3 removed it; token_binding_id_b64 is ignored
  - requested_uv must be required, preferred or discouraged; any other
    value, which used to mean preferred, is refused

  [Fixes]
  - Client-supplied CBOR is decoded by this module, in time and memory
    bounded by its size. CBOR::XS calls THAW on a value tagged as a Perl
    object, and expands string references, so that a few kilobytes
    become gigabytes. Only the CBOR WebAuthn uses is accepted: no tags,
    floats, indefinite lengths or duplicate map keys, and no text string
    where bytes are expected or the reverse; COSE key types, algorithms
    and curves must be integers
  - An RSA credential key over 4096 bits, or with an even modulus or an
    invalid exponent, is refused before it is read, which took up to a
    minute for a large one. The 2048-bit minimum counts bits, not bytes
  - PS256 signatures did not verify (wrong salt length)
  - trust_anchors given as a code reference was always refused
  - Ed25519 attestation certificates were refused
  - Malformed input is refused with this module's own messages, not Perl
    errors and warnings; this includes a packed attestation certificate
    without Basic Constraints
  - An appid extension output is ignored if the appid extension was not
    requested, when requested_extensions is given
  - An attestation statement format that is a CBOR byte string, not a
    text string, is refused
  - Authenticator data with Backup State but not Backup Eligibility, and
    credential IDs over 1023 bytes, are refused, as WebAuthn Level 3
    requires
  - A trust anchor that is not a PEM certificate is refused, naming it.
    It used to make every chain fail, or with allow_untrusted_attestation
    make every attestation Self without a word
  - An attestation certificate's curve must match the attestation
    algorithm (ES256 with a P-384 key was accepted)
  - fido-u2f attestation must carry exactly one certificate, and an
    id-fido-gen-ce-aaguid extension marked critical is refused
  - Bytes after a DER attestation certificate are refused

  [New]
  - ES384, ES512 and EdDSA (Ed25519) credential keys, and RFC 9864's
    fully-specified ESP256, ESP384, ESP512, Ed25519 and Ed448 identifiers
  - origin can be an arrayref of accepted origins (Frank Carnovale)
  - generate_registration_options and generate_assertion_options, which
    check their options against the WebAuthn enumerations and take hints
    and extensions, and offer EdDSA, ES256 and RS256 by default
  - allowed_algs option to validate_registration
  - mediation option to validate_registration: with conditional
    (automatic passkey upgrade), User Present is not required
  - credential_json: validate_registration and validate_assertion take
    the credential as the browser serializes it (PublicKeyCredential
    .toJSON()) in place of its parts; registration then returns the
    transports the browser reported
  - allow_sign_count_regression: a signature count that did not increase
    is reported as sign_count_regressed instead of failing, for Relying
    Parties whose policy is to flag the credential
  - appid option to validate_assertion, for U2F AppIDs other than the
    origin
  - validate_registration and validate_assertion return user_verified,
    backup_eligible and backup_state
  - Sign-in without a username: validate_assertion checks the credential
    against the allowed credentials and the user handle against the
    credential's owner, and can require a user handle
  - The apple, android-key and tpm attestation formats, checked against
    the specification's test vectors; android_key_require_tee accepts
    only keys the trusted execution environment enforces
  - Extension outputs are checked: the client's (appid, appidExclude,
    credProps, largeBlob, prf) and the authenticator's (credProtect,
    hmac-secret, minPinLength, credBlob), against their types and against
    what was requested (prf asks for hmac-secret and hmac-secret-mc); a
    malformed output nobody requested is left out.
    validate_registration reports whether the credential is discoverable

  [Authen::WebAuthn::Test]
  - Produces RSA, P-384, P-521, Ed25519 and Ed448 keys
  - Deterministic: ECDSA signatures follow RFC 6979 and client data is
    encoded with its keys in order, so the same inputs give the same
    response
  - Packed attestation, self or with a certificate, and fido-u2f
    attestation
  - Encodes CBOR canonically, as authenticators do, with integer map
    keys as CBOR integers
  - encode_credential encodes userHandle, and a registration's rawId, as
    base64url

  [Tests]
  - A test PKI in t/pki, and tests reaching every statement
  - The test vectors of the WebAuthn specification (t/spec-vectors), run
    by t/21-Spec-test-vectors.t
  - xt/differential.t verifies the same responses, valid and corrupted,
    with py_webauthn, and fails on any disagreement not explained
  - xt/valgrind.t runs the tests under valgrind's memcheck when it is
    installed
  - GitHub Actions runs the tests on Perl 5.20 to 5.40, and under
    valgrind

0.005
  - Add allow_unknown_attestation_format option

0.004
  - Add allow_untrusted_attestation option

0.003 2023-11-09
  - Update Crypt::OpenSSL::X509::Extension and Net::SSLeay dependancies
  - Make sure Net::SSLeay is initialized before use

0.002 2023-11-09
  - Supported attestation types: none, packed, fido-u2f
  - Mandatory attestation trust chain validation

0.001 2022-01-21
  - initial release
  - Authentication and registration without attestation
  - ECC/RSA keys supported
